        "KNaO": {
          "min": 0.15,
          "max": 0.35
        },
        "P2O5": {
          "min": 0.01,
          "max": 0.03
        }
      },
      "si_al_ratio": {
        "min": 10.5,
        "max": 13.0
      },
      "required_colorants": [
        {
          "oxide": "Fe2O3",
          "min_pct": 1.0,
          "max_pct": 1.5,
          "source_material": "Red Iron Oxide"
        }
      ],
      "preferred_materials": [
        "Custer Feldspar",
        "Whiting",
        "Bone Ash",
        "Silica (Flint)",
        "EPK Kaolin"
      ],
      "material_constraints": {
        "Bone Ash": {
          "min_pct": 0.5,
          "max_pct": 3.0
        }
      },
      "notes": "Si:Al is the critical number - a typical stoneware glaze sits near 8:1 and will not separate. Low alumina is what permits separation, so keep Al2O3 near 0.30. Iron at 1-1.5% is needed for the blue to read despite the effect being optical, and TiO2 above ~0.2% turns it green. Phosphorus is beneficial but not the cause of the opalescence - modern analysis attributes its contribution to driving bubble-size variation, which amplifies the scattering. Apply thickly: thin chun lets body alumina dissolve in, which pulls the composition off the immiscibility boundary and fires amber clear. Sometimes called Jun."
    },
