          "oxide": "SnO2",
          "min_pct": 1.0,
          "max_pct": 1.5,
          "source_material": "Tin Oxide"
        }
      ],
      "preferred_materials": [
        "Custer Feldspar",
        "Silica (Flint)",
        "Whiting (Calcium Carbonate)",
        "EPK Kaolin"
      ],
      "material_constraints": {},
      "notes": "Reduction is not a preference here, it is the mechanism - the red is colloidal metallic copper, and it dies on reoxidation. Keep copper carbonate at or below 0.5%: above that the colour goes liver, metallic or straight green, and the corpus median sits at 0.49% right against that ceiling. Tin nucleates the colloid, so it is required rather than an opacifier here - roughly 1% tin to 0.33% copper (Pinnell). Start reduction early, around cone 012, so the glaze is reduced before it seals over. In a wood or soda kiln where the atmosphere swings, 0.25-0.5% silicon carbide in the copper layer reduces locally regardless of what the kiln is doing. Copper volatilises and will flash neighbouring pots pink and green from several shelves away - warn whoever shares the chamber."
    }
